# The full container run at e75ee6c7: 39 gates, 31 594 tests, tree listing kept (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full` was run end to end against the committed tree at
`e75ee6c7` and passed: 39 gates, 386 test-result blocks, 31 594 tests passed, 485 ignored,
0 failed, exit 0. It is the successor to
`docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-c8104865-2026-10-04.log` and is the record behind
the `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` row of `scripts/ci/docker-labs.tsv`.

Two things in this run are new since that one, because `e75ee6c7` is the commit that
changed them. The fingerprint line now carries a second digest, over which paths were
listed rather than only what they contain, and the per-path listings behind both checksums
can be kept instead of deleted. Section 1 uses both, and section 5 states what the run
still does not say.

== 0. Provenance of the run

The script bind-mounts the repository root it was invoked from and fingerprints the working
tree once before the gates and once after, so the run went to a self-contained clone rather
than the live checkout:

    $ git clone /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7
    $ cd /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7 && git checkout --detach e75ee6c7
    $ git rev-parse HEAD
    e75ee6c76a06195fb3ceca0276f994ab3ab66417
    $ git status --porcelain | wc -l
    0

    $ cd /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7
    $ CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST=/var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7-manifest \
        ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full > /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log 2>&1
    $ echo "exit=$?"
    exit=0

`git worktree` is not an option here: the script's first action is a preflight in which the
container reads the mounted `/src` with git, and a worktree's `.git` is a text pointer to a
file inside the parent repository that does not exist inside the container. The empty
`git status` before the run is what makes the checksum below a claim about `e75ee6c7` and
not about some working state.

== 1. The verdict, and what the attribution line can now be asked

    == building chaos-verify:local from docker.io/library/debian:bookworm-slim
    == preparing cargo cache volumes
    == source tree: /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7 at e75ee6c7: 4032 files, checksum 3344101559
       (path set 3492843690)

    == preflight: container can read the repository
    preflight: OK

    ...

    GUI protocol types are up to date

    all gates passed in chaos-verify:local

The transcript never prints `UNATTRIBUTABLE`, which is the statement that all 39 gates, and
the 31 594 tests inside them, belong to the tree whose contents checksum is 3344101559 and
whose path set is 3492843690. The two digests are read differently: the first moves when a
tracked file's bytes move, the second moves when a path is added or removed even if the sum
of bytes is unchanged. A previous log had to argue from the file count that the path sets
matched; here the path-set digest is the direct measurement of that.

`CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST` is what makes the numbers checkable afterwards. The run kept four
files, the two listings taken 31 minutes apart at either end of the sweep:

    $ ls -l /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7-manifest
    total 1080
    -rw-rw----  1 chaos chaos 239313 Oct  4 20:56 after.list
    -rw-rw----  1 chaos chaos 304016 Oct  4 20:56 after.sums
    -rw-rw----  1 chaos chaos 239313 Oct  4 20:25 before.list
    -rw-rw----  1 chaos chaos 304016 Oct  4 20:25 before.sums

    $ cd /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7-manifest
    $ cmp before.list after.list && echo "before.list == after.list"
    before.list == after.list
    $ cmp before.sums after.sums && echo "before.sums == after.sums"
    before.sums == after.sums

Those four files are the whole reason a non-reproducing checksum is diagnosable rather than
merely disappointing: they say which paths were listed and what each hashed to, at both ends
of the sweep. Without the variable, both listings are written under a temporary directory
the script's own `EXIT` trap removes, which is how the predecessor of this record lost its
evidence. The manifest was requested for this run precisely because the previous sweep's
checksum had failed to reproduce from its named clone, and the row describing that
(`TODO.md`, the attribution row) was settled by measuring exactly this instrument.

The transcript was created at 20:25:28 and last written at 20:56:30, so 1 862 seconds
(31 minutes 02 seconds). That is not a cold-cache figure: the cargo registry, the cargo git
checkouts and `target/` live in three named volumes earlier runs had already filled, and
`chaos-verify:local` was rebuilt from the same Dockerfile rather than from nothing.

== 2. What ran, and how the gate count is derived

`--full` prints no gate count, so the number is derived rather than quoted. The script holds
37 gates as literal entries in its `gates=()` array and appends two more only in full mode:

    $ awk 'NR>230 && /^\)/{exit} NR>230 && /^  "/{n++} END {print "literal gates:", n+0}' \
          scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    literal gates: 37
    $ grep -n 'gates+=(' scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    374:  gates+=("cargo test: ${bootstrap}; cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast")
    380:  gates+=("GUI protocol types: ${bootstrap}; bash scripts/ci/check-gui-protocol.sh")

Both appended lines sit inside `if [ "${MODE}" = "full" ]`, so a `--full` run executes 39
gates. The transcript carries 46 lines starting with `== `: those 39 labels, 4 preamble lines
(`building`, `preparing cargo cache volumes`, `source tree:`, `preflight:`) and 3
sub-headings that two gates print for themselves. That arithmetic is the check that no gate
silently ran twice or not at all.

Four gates print a count worth recording, because they read the whole tree:

    ignored-test baseline: 429 total, 0 approved bare, 0 new
    baseline holds: 97 crates, 0 fewer production sites than recorded
    uncompiled set holds: 0 files, exactly as recorded
    metric labels hold: 83 registrations, 155 label-value call sites, 0 recorded
    timeout children hold: 12 timeout-abandoned output/status site(s), 12 kill their child
      on drop, 0 are std commands, 0 recorded
    tree-ownership: OK (9731 path(s) walked under src, all owned by 1003, 1 path(s) skipped
      as pruned or mounted)
    check-lab-coverage: OK (6 lab(s): 3 run by CI, 3 with a current ledger row)

== 3. The cargo legs

    $ grep -nE '^== (cargo|GUI)' /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log
    61:== cargo fmt
    1096:== cargo check
    1205:== cargo clippy
    1309:== cargo test
    36192:== GUI protocol types

`cargo check` finished in 3m 27s and `cargo clippy` in 3m 38s. Clippy runs with
`-D warnings`, and the region between its heading and the next carries zero lines starting
with `warning`:

    $ awk '/^== cargo clippy/,/^== cargo test/' /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log \
          | grep -c '^warning'
    0

So the test counts below come from a tree that also compiles clean under the lint set, and a
passing test is not standing in for a build problem somewhere else.

== 4. The size of the test leg

    $ grep -c "^test result: ok" /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log
    386
    $ grep -c "^test result: FAILED" /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log
    0
    $ grep -oE "^test result: ok\. [0-9]+ passed; [0-9]+ failed; [0-9]+ ignored" \
          /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log | awk -F'[ ;]+' \
          '{p+=$4; f+=$6; i+=$8} END {print "passed:", p, " failed:", f, " ignored:", i}'
    passed: 31594  failed: 0  ignored: 485

The leg runs with `--no-fail-fast`, so "0 failed" is a conclusion drawn after all 386 blocks
ran rather than a sweep that stopped at the first red test. The 485 ignored split as 436 unit
and integration tests plus 49 doctests, which cargo reports in the same line shape:

    $ grep "\.\.\. ignored" /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log | grep -v "\.rs - " | wc -l
    436
    $ grep -E "^test [^ ]+\.rs - .* \.\.\. ignored" /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7.log | wc -l
    49

That is a different population from the `ignored-test baseline` gate's "429 total", which
counts `#[ignore]` attributes in the tracked sources and so sees neither the doctests nor an
attribute the scan cannot attach to a test function.

== 5. Five fixture cases that declined to run inside the container

The run is green, and four of its own fixture suites still declined five cases:

    Ran 9 tests in 0.408s
    OK (skipped=1)                                    workflow yaml
        skipped 'PyYAML is not installed on this host'
    Ran 28 tests in 0.934s
    OK (skipped=1)                                    tree ownership
        skipped 'root reads a 000 directory, so the branch cannot be reached here'
    Ran 33 tests in 16.930s
    OK (skipped=1)                                    metric labels
        skipped 'the prometheus source is not vendored on this host'
    Ran 38 tests in 28.800s
    OK (skipped=2)                                    timeout children
        skipped 'the pinned tokio source is not vendored on this machine'   (x2)

None of the five is a hole this commit could close by editing a test. Two need a vendored
crate source the image does not carry (`prometheus` for the metric-labels premise, `tokio`
for the two timeout-children premises); one needs PyYAML, which the image deliberately omits
so that `check-workflow-yaml.py`'s own line-oriented reader stays the thing under test; one
needs a host where the test user is not root, and the container user is root, so a `chmod
000` directory is readable there and the branch that reports "cannot read the tree" cannot be
reached at all. All five run where their precondition holds, which is why the premises they
check are still checked somewhere.

== 6. What this record is not

- It is `e75ee6c7`, the commit before the workspace undo point. The engine, host and browser
  tests that commit does not contain are recorded, with their own mutation measurements, in
  `docs/verification/workspace-diff-undo-2026-10-04.log`. Anything added after it is outside
  this sweep's scope, and the next sweep will move the ledger row again.
- Not a cold cache, and not a baseline. See section 1 about the named volumes.
- Linux only. The entry point is a Debian bookworm-slim container; the macOS and Windows
  conclusions come from their own runners, recorded in
  `docs/verification/platform-ci-2026-10-02.log` and
  `docs/verification/windows-test-failures-2026-10-04.log`.
- No browser, and no network egress from the gates themselves. Nothing in the 39 gates drives
  a UI: the image has no node_modules and no browser binary, so "all gates passed" and "the
  desktop flows behave" remain two separate conclusions.

To reproduce it:

    $ git clone /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7
    $ cd /var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7 && git checkout --detach e75ee6c7
    $ CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST=/var/tmp/full-e75ee6c7-manifest ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full
